.TH REALPATH 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
realpath \- print the canonical absolute name of each file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B realpath
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B realpath
prints the canonical absolute name of each
.IR file ,
one per line, on standard output.
A canonical name has no
.B .\&
or
.B ..\&
components, no repeated separators, and no symbolic links or junctions.
It is written with slashes, as in
.BR C:/Users/me/src ,
except where noted below.
.PP
There must be at least one
.IR file .
Standard input is not read.
The operands are processed in order.
One that cannot be resolved is reported on standard error, and the rest
are still processed.
.SS Existing files
Resolution has two steps.
The first works on the text of the operand, without touching the file
system.
It makes the name absolute, relative to the current directory, and
removes
.B .\&
components, repeated separators, and each
.B ..\&
with the component before it.
The empty operand is the current directory, a name that begins with a
single
.B /
or
.B \e
starts at the root of the current drive, and a drive without a
separator, as in
.BR C:dir ,
is relative to the current directory on that drive.
As elsewhere in Windows, trailing dots and spaces in a component are
ignored.
.PP
Because this step removes
.B ..\&
by text, it does not follow links first:
.B link/..\&
is the directory that contains
.BR link ,
not the parent of its target, and
.B missing/..\&
succeeds although
.B missing
does not exist.
.PP
The second step opens the resulting name, which fails unless the file
exists, and asks Windows for its final name.
This replaces every symbolic link and junction with its target, expands
8.3 short names, writes the drive letter in upper case, and gives every
component the case it has on disk.
An app execution alias, one of the program names that Windows keeps in a
user's
.I WindowsApps
directory, is replaced by the program it runs.
The result has no
.B \e\e?\e
prefix, and a name on a network share is written
.IR //server/share/dir .
Backslashes become slashes, and a trailing separator is removed unless
it ends a drive root such as
.BR C:/ .
An existing file followed by a separator is an error.
.SS Files that do not exist
A name that does not exist is still printed when only its last component
is missing, so that the name of a file to be created can be resolved.
The operand is split at its last separator, after trailing separators
are removed.
The part before it is resolved as above, and the last component is
appended as written, in its own case, after the separator that
was in the operand.
That separator may be a backslash, so in
.IR C:/work ,
.B dir\enew
prints as
.BR C:/work/dir\enew .
.PP
It is an error if the part before the separator does not exist, or if
the operand has no separator.
So
.B new
fails, and
.B ./new
does not.
The part before the separator need not be a directory.
.PP
Two cases differ.
When the last separator is the one that ends the root of the operand, as
in
.BR C:/new ,
.BR /new ,
or
.BR //host/share/new ,
there is nothing to resolve, and the operand is printed as written,
without being made absolute.
So
.B /new
prints
.BR /new ,
as does
.BR ///new .
And when the part before the separator resolves to a drive root, the
result has two separators after the drive, as in
.BR C://new .
.PP
A symbolic link or junction whose target does not exist is replaced by
its target, and the rules above apply to that.
A relative target is taken relative to the current directory, not to the
directory that holds the link.
.SH OPTIONS
.B realpath
takes no options, so an operand that begins with a dash, such as
.BR \-s ,
is a
.IR file ,
and so is
.BR \-\- .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
When it is
.BR 1 ,
Windows shows its critical-error dialogs, such as for a drive with no
disk in it, instead of the operation failing with an error.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
the C runtime expands the wildcards
.B *
and
.B ?\&
in the last component of each argument when busybox starts, even in
quoted arguments, as needed for commands typed in
.BR cmd.exe .
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is, and then fails as
described under
.BR NOTES .
When the variable is unset, busybox sets it to
.BR 0 ,
so that busybox programs it starts do not expand their arguments again.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, and the console interprets the
bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox program, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every operand was resolved and printed.
.TP
.B 1
There was no operand, with a usage message on standard error, or at
least one operand could not be resolved, or standard output could not be
written.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader exited before
.B realpath
finished writing.
Nothing is printed.
When this is first detected as the output is flushed at exit, the
status is 1 instead.
.PP
A failure is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
realpath: \fIfile\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I reason
is the system's message for the error, such as
.B No such file or directory
or
.BR "Invalid argument" .
A write error names
.B standard output
in place of the file.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the canonical name of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cd C:/work/app
$ realpath ../lib/./util.c
C:/work/lib/util.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Expand a short name:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ realpath c:/progra~1/common~1
C:/Program Files/Common Files
.EE
.RE
.PP
Name a file that does not exist yet, in a directory that does:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ realpath build/app.exe
C:/work/app/build/app.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Test whether the directory of a new file exists:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ realpath nodir/log.txt || echo cannot resolve
realpath: nodir/log.txt: No such file or directory
cannot resolve
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR realpath :
.IP \(bu 2
There are no options, such as
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-z ,
or
.BR \-\-relative\-to .
.IP \(bu 2
A missing last component is allowed, as with GNU
.BR realpath ,
but only when the operand has a separator.
.IP \(bu 2
The empty operand is the current directory, not an error.
.IP \(bu 2
.B ..\&
is resolved before links, not after.
.IP \(bu 2
Results are not made absolute in every case, and may mix separators; see
.BR "Files that do not exist" .
.PP
A name containing
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
.BR < ,
.BR > ,
.BR | ,
or
.BR \(dq ,
as an unmatched wildcard does, fails with
.BR "Invalid argument" ,
and so does a name that Windows takes for a device, such as
.B NUL
or a bare
.BR CON .
.PP
Names are limited to 259 characters.
An operand of 260 or more characters, as typed or once made absolute, is
treated as if its last component were missing, and if the part before
the last separator is too long as well,
.B realpath
fails with
.BR "No such file or directory" .
When the final name of an existing file would have 256 or more
characters, or 254 on a network share, the second step only follows a
link in the last component:
names keep the case and form typed, and other links are not followed.
.PP
On Windows XP, which lacks the API for finding a final name, the second
step only checks that the file exists and follows a link in the last
component, so names keep the case and form typed.
.PP
Started from
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B realpath *.c
prints every match, as described under
.BR BB_GLOBBING .
In the kit's shell, the shell expands the pattern.
.PP
On Windows 10 version 1903 and later, a name may contain any Unicode
characters.
On older versions it is limited to the ANSI code page.
.PP
Lines end with LF, not CRLF.
When standard output is a console, the output is converted for display
from the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later.
.PP
The kit's
.B sh
runs
.B realpath
inside the shell process, without starting a new one, except where the
shell needs a separate process anyway, as for a command substitution or
a pipeline.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR basename (1),
.BR busybox (1),
.BR dirname (1),
.BR jn (1),
.BR ln (1),
.BR readlink (1),
.BR sh (1)
